s entities in our system  and when state changes occur  all these entities needs to be updated  transactionally  as an example once the driver accepts  the offer  the rider also needs to be associated  with that driver  and all the downstream systems needs to  be updated and kept in sync  also the presented offer needs to auto  expire  if no action has been taken after few  seconds and the feedback for this  also needs to be propagated to  downstream systems  these entities are modeled as state  machines as it undergoes  various entity state transitions from  start to finish of a trip  now this is a very simple example of a  two-sided marketplace  involving a driver and a rider the  complexity increases  with eads which is a three-sided  marketplace involving  a restaurant a delivery person and an  eater  also with some advanced use cases like  trying to batch  more trips together for products like  uberpool and each deliveries  the fulfillment platform handles all  these real-time use cases  so more than a year back we took a  holistic look at our architecture with  an aim to simplify and modernize them  let's look at how we approached we  categorized our challenges  into the following dimensions one was  focused on platform efficiency  and other on data management on platform  efficiency a lot of product teams across  uber  use the fulfillment platform to build  business features  we have to ensure that we build  extensible components across  all our product lines without  introducing complexity  at the same time we want to make sure we  maximize  on our infrastructure efficiency by  improving throughput per core  given the scale of uber even an  incremental improvement here  can have a meaningful impact on the  overall infrastructure spend  from data management point of view our  current application  architecture relies on an eventually  consistent model  and adds complexity at the application  layer to deal with consistency  also when you have so many microservices  state management across  all of them is a huge challenge for our  systems to be reliable we need a  strongly consistent and an available  database  that can scale to our needs and can also  support  complex transactions at scale to  overcome these constraints  we use solutions at application level  like maintaining transaction queues  sticky sharding  along with leveraging ring pop for  coordination  and some patterns around transaction  orchestration  but if you think about it these  application level complexity can be  simplified  with pushing some of them to the  database level  so we classified our database  requirements further into system and  usability aspects  from systems requirement we wanted an  asset compliance system  which can give us finings of  availability and comes with globally  consistent guarantees  uber has multiple data centers so the  solution  should work across data centers and  should satisfy our latency  sls from a usability standpoint we  wanted  support for relational modeling based on  the previous example  we wanted support for a multi-faceted  marketplace where business entities are  highly relational  it should have good observability for  tracking and tracing issues  and should be easy to scale as the  business grows  so with these requirements which are  super high level  in mind the team looked at different  database solutions  we chose cloud spanner for evaluation as  it satisfied all our core requirements  as we started ideating our solution on  top of spanner  we went through a process of identifying  the gaps of what we needed to build  before we could start using spanner  here are some of the things which we  build specific to our use case  pro programming model is highly  asynchronous  and we wanted all our spanner rpcs to be  asynchronous too  for this we built an async spanner  client  so that it can fit into our execution  model  a background task execution system we  built an event sourcing solution on top  of spanner  which we will look into greater detail  further down  ttl as part of a real-time platform  we needed support for ttl so once a trip  ends or an order is fulfilled the data  gets handled by a post-trip  processing platform ttl support is  needed so the data can be cleaned up  once it's no longer needed by the  real-time platform  a transaction execution framework we've  optimized on different strategies for  executing our transactions  and also build tooling around detecting  block contentions and deadlock  lastly for monitoring since all our  metrics are persisted in our internal  systems  some of which are also open source like  n3 and jaeger  we wanted spanner server side metrics  also to be available  internally and closely integrated with  our monitoring and alerting systems  let's look at some of them more closely  we started off with a very lightweight  spanner client  generated using google's open source api  generator  and built on top of grpc this is a java  client  and as mentioned previously this aligns  closely  and well with our async programming  pattern  and also gives us a better handle for  tuning client-side resources  we added more functionality to the  client as part of session  and transaction management to give some  more context  a session acts as a communication  channel and you can only run one  transaction at a time  on a session they are reusable resources  so we build session pooling from ground  up which can auto scale based on usage  for transactions we built different  transaction execution strategies to  reduce our transaction time  more on this later with an example on  observability friend  we wanted to integrate it closely with  our internal observability platforms  like  logging metrics and tracing  to talk more on other systems and  solutions we built  i will hand over to nanda who will take  it from here  thanks ram to give you some context  we wanted a system for executing  background async tasks with at least one  guarantee  we were looking for an event sourcing  solution on top of spanner  such a solution would be used for things  like publishing  internal state change events or to kick  off poster processing like sending  customer notifications for things like  ratings etc  these tasks would be registered as part  of the same transaction as other  entities  for example if the driver has ended the  trip  the task to do post strip processing  would also be registered as part of the  same  interrupt transaction moreover  these tasks also needed to have a timer  property  or they can be scheduled to execute at  some time in future  or they can be scheduled to execute  tasks periodically  to build such a system we introduced a  task table where we  can store all the registered tasks we  then built an efficient scanner  also known as tailors that scans through  this table  and schedules them in memory for  execution  they were named as tailors inspired by  the unix terminology  as the scans only look for new data we  were able to horizontally scale the  system by having multiple tailors across  different shards  so that each scan got its own key space  this approach allowed us to detect any  new tasks for background execution  within milliseconds of them being  registered  another system that we built was a  spanner monitoring service  which is a standalone monitoring service  that imports critical stack driver  metrics into our internal monitoring  stack  the reason why we did why we built this  was to have spanner monitoring well  integrated with our internal systems  especially paging and metric analysis  some of the analysis that we were able  to perform were  session analysis that is to check if  sessions are properly reused and for how  long  than transaction analysis to analyze  long-running transactions  that is spanner provides interesting  stats which allows  us to introspect and dive deeper into  queries and dmls taking longer time  we get this data from spanner and index  it internally making it easier to search  and  analyze building a spanner monitoring  service allowed us to build  also allowed us to build an auto scaler  functionality recently  to scale spanner nodes in an elastic way  based on various server side parameters  like spanner cpu utilization  and storage  while building these systems and testing  spanner at scale we came across some  learnings that we would like to share  that helped us along the way  let's dive deeper technically  the first thing is request patterns so  it's important to have good  understanding of request patterns in  order to come up with the right schema  it is also equally important to  understand how spanner performs reads  and writes  for such a pattern for us we had three  types of patterns  simple crud like operations on a given  row  for such cases completely random primary  key  like uuid works really great it gives us  the best possible distribution and also  avoids hot spots  for multi-row updates or for accessing  related items  use interleave tables it gives locality  with parent rows  ensuring your joints are efficient  the other use case as mentioned before  was to build an efficient scanner that  scans through new rows  frequent road deletions in your table  can cause tombstones which can make your  scans  inefficient causing higher scan  latencies  for such use cases a combination of  shard id  and spanner timestamp works really well  the problem with timestamps in a key  either primary or secondary is that it  can cause hot spots  using shard id prevents the hotspots and  at the same time allows multiple workers  to scan  individual shards in parallel  there were quite a few learnings for us  with respect to how we do transactions  on spanner  so first one was to optimize for  reducing read write transaction time  read write transaction time is measured  as the time between  the first operation in a transaction to  the final operation which is often the  commit  in general increase and read write  transaction time  is equal to increase block contention  and consequently increase in abort rate  in the system  when we started out we had a lot of  aborted transactions because  of log contentions to have deeper  understanding we built a transaction  analyzer tool to identify overlapping  transactions  and dig deeper into each contention  whether it is systemic or whether it's  because of poor request pattern etc  this allowed us to identify the type of  transactions that can be retried on a  bot and the ones that should not  this was important as the last thing you  want is all containing transactions  retrying forever causing more churn and  contentions  for read-only transactions use snapshot  reads wherever possible to increase your  read performance  this allows the reads to be served from  any replicas and consequently reduce the  load on the  leader let's look at an optimization  story with respect to transaction  execution  in the first graph you can see we  started off  with a basic read modified write example  where we read  then write and finally commit  in all there are there were three round  trips to spanner not including the  initial begin transaction as we have a  pool of transactions ready to go  over time our flows got more complex  where we often had to do multiple reads  and writes within a transaction  thus increasing the number of round  trips to spanner  now why does round trip to spanner  matter  depending on where your data center is  each spanner round trip is additional  network latency for example if a  transaction has  five round trips and if network latency  is 30 milliseconds you have lost 150  milliseconds just to the network  as we highlighted earlier uh keeping a  transaction  open for longer also increases the  chances of contention  so to solve for this we systematically  broke down the transaction  by identifying the paths of transaction  that can happen in parallel by building  a transaction graph this allowed us to  issue reads in parallel but writes were  still serialized as the dmls needed to  be in sequence  as illustrated in graph 3 this allowed  us to  reduce the round trips to spanner by  just parallelizing reads  to solve for rights we batched all  rights  and commit together in a single rpc  using the mutations api  as provided by spanner to look at  other learnings with respect to spanner  client side  and server side tunings i will hand it  over to ram  thanks nanda so here are some of the  considerations for client-side  performance and tuning  we'll look at two main aspects to tune  on the client side  sessions and grpc we spoke about  sessions  and session pool as part of session pool  configurations  we recommend to maintain or always  maintain necessary ratio of read write  sessions versus sessions that are used  only for reads a read write session  is a session associated with the  transaction using the begin transaction  api  you can size your session pool based on  the expected workload  and transaction rate also  session management apis like create  sessions and batch create sessions  have high latency it's better to  pre-provision sessions instead of  incurring cost during transaction  operations  on the grpc front we always need to  ensure  to set appropriate deadlines to the grpc  context  we also learned that commit should be  given more time to finish  compared to other operations  for flow control grpc streams are  bi-directional streams  and are mapped to http 2 streams  payloads are serialized as byte streams  which are then fragmented into http 2  frames  grpc uses flow control mechanism in http  2  and this also helps in finer grain  control  for buffering in-flight messages  we optimized our flow control window  depending on our payload size  which we found during our test  compression  this needs to be carefully chosen based  on the payload size  we found our proto payloads are highly  compressible  leading to bandwidth savings currently  only gzip is supported as part of the  grpc  compression codec we recommend on  leveraging grpc  interceptors for observability this can  be plugged into the client for  introspection and tracing  from a server-side tuning point of view  based on our testing we could scale  spanner horizontally  one of the core metric for scaling  spanner nodes is cpu usage  it's recommended to operate below 75  percent  on a smooth cpu usage spanner  operational management is simple  and it's easy to provision more nodes as  and when you need it  it's also important to choose  appropriate regions as spanner offers  variety of options  like namthry namsex and regional spanner  instances  on the network side since we run our  infrastructure  in our own data center it's important to  have an optimized routing strategy to  span a server  we work with gcp to make this happen  on the database workload spanner  performs database splits and compactions  based on traffic this can be a load or a  size based split  which might have a brief impact on  latency it's important to have an  appropriate ramp up strategy  so that there is minimal disruptions  during the split  with all these learnings and  optimizations and along with the support  of the systems we built  we performed simulations on spanner with  a synthetic  uber scale load before we look at these  numbers  it's important to understand these are  strictly dependent on data modeling  and how transactions are run this is  highly use case specific  and might not apply to every use case  here are the following highlights we are  able to scale spanner nodes horizontally  to more than 600 nodes with a 330 000  per second reads per second and 130  000 writes per second  cpu usage stayed close to 67 percent  and in terms of latency we mainly cared  about the following rpcs  execute sql execute batch dml and commit  latency  which were within our acceptable latency  requirements  we were also working on a bunch of more  optimizations to perform  better the performance  so what are we doing next we're looking  towards building out new features  like forecasting or spanner nodes based  on  predicted load and optimize on cpu usage  we're also looking forward to testing  few spanner features like  backup and restore we will also look at  open sourcing some of our work  in the future lastly  as we are working on productionizing our  platform on cloud spanner there's a  whole team behind this  and lots of people are involved both on  uber and from google cloud team  as we embark on this journey we are  looking forward to this continued  collaboration  and partnership on behalf of nanda  i thank you for watching the talk

